To let the kernel know how to unlock the root partition, we need to add the
relevant option to /etc/crypttab.initramfs, which will be packaged into the UKI.
We have covered crypttab.initramfs before but here is an overview of its four
columns as a reminder:
    - volume-name: the name of the mapping the unlocked volume will take
    - encrypted device: the path to the encrypted volume or its device UUID if
        starting with "UUID="
    - key-file: the path to the key-file; can be "none" or "-" if unlocked via
        password entry or a method specified in the next field
    - options: options for unlocking the volume, separated by comma

The last field ("options") is where we specify which of our enrolled hardware
tokens can be used to unlock the root partition. Only one token has been added
to the file but you may add additional tokens separated by comma, if you wish.
systemd will attempt to validate them in the order provided. The available
options corresponding to their respective devices are:
    - TPM2: tpm2-device=<device>
    - FIDO2: fido2-device=<device>
    - PKCS#11: pkcs11-token-uri=<uri>

One thing to keep in mind is that the volume-name field MUST be set to a mapping
name that corresponds to the root parameter on the kernel commandline (set in
/etc/kernel/cmdline). For example, this parameter set on the commandline
root=/dev/mapper/cryptroot
would require setting the volume-name column to "cryptroot".
If you are using systemd-automounting and are not setting the root parameter on
the kernel commandline, volume-name must instead be set to "root".

The script will take care of filling in the right name but it is mentioned here
nonetheless as putting in a wrong name is a common mistake that will result in
an unbootable system.